                                                                          Layer 2
                                                                      ESP-32 Sentinel
The Problem: 
Satellites (Layer 0) are great at detecting massive fires or heat signatures, but their resolution is poor (often 1 pixel = 1 square kilometer). Furthermore, satellites can be blocked by heavy clouds, and sometimes false positive heat reflections occur. We cannot launch a drone based solely on a satellite ping.

The Solution: 
The ESP-32 Sentinels act as the Ground Truth. They are scattered throughout the hazard zone.

They spend almost their entire life asleep, preserving battery for years.

When Layer 0 (NASA) yells "WAKE UP" via MQTT, the ESP-32s wake up instantly and turn on their local sensors.

If the sensors detect extreme heat or light flashes, they confirm that the macro-threat seen from space is actually happening in the forest at exact GPS coordinates.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Implementation:
The Implementation Flow:

The ESP32 connects to the local Wi-Fi (acting as the stand-in for a production LoRaWAN network).

It connects to the MQTT broker (e.g., Mosquitto running on your laptop or HiveMQ public broker).

It subscribes to the topic helios/zoneA/commands.

The "Hack" for the Demo: True Deep Sleep on an ESP32 shuts down the Wi-Fi modem, meaning it cannot receive MQTT messages. 
For a real production build, you use a hardware interrupt pin connected to a separate ultra-low-power LoRa receiver. 
For the 5-day hackathon demo, you will just program the ESP32 to sit in an idle delay() loop where it doesn't read the sensors until it receives the MQTT message.
This simulates the power-saving workflow perfectly for the judges.
